WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.760
Speaker 1: Let's go next&nbsp;
to let's go to Brendan from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:09.120
Jersey. Is it Brendan or Brenda? It's cut off.
Speaker 4: Oh, it's fine. It's Brendan.&nbsp;

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:12.240
Speaker 1: Brendan. Okay, welcome.
Speaker 4: Thanks for taking my call.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:17.920
So my two topics for a question would be a&nbsp;
2024 question for the Dems and a question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:23.280
about economics that could be agreed upon.
Speaker 3: By both sides. Okay. Which which of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:26.160
those would you think would be more interesting?
Speaker 1: Let's do economics.&nbsp;

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:31.360
Speaker 4: So the first one would be with the&nbsp;
development of AI and robotics and lack of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:37.520
general skilled labor. Trending over time, is it&nbsp;
time to properly incentivize, de-stigmatize and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:41.120
promote vocational schools for skills.
Speaker 3: And trade development,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:44.160
things like that?
Speaker 1: 100%. I mean, listen, I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:50.800
I'm not someone who just says college is&nbsp;
for everyone. College doesn't have to be for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:56.000
everyone. And in fact, I think the statistics are&nbsp;
that most people still don't don't go to college.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:01.760
There's a couple different things.&nbsp;
One, if everyone has a college degree,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:08.000
the college degree is no longer a filter&nbsp;
for certain jobs, and then you just need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:14.000
something else. Is it now graduate school? Is&nbsp;
it whatever? So. So in one sense, college can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:20.160
be the right path for some jobs and other paths&nbsp;
make sense for other jobs. In addition to that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:25.760
particularly if we are going to see the&nbsp;
technological change and advances that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:32.880
we see in energy, in fabrication, in materials&nbsp;
sciences, all these different things. We want as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:39.680
many people as possible. We need way more people&nbsp;
getting involved in the trades in order to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:44.400
ready for those coming changes and to get younger&nbsp;
people. A lot of these trades skew significantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:48.720
older and it's great now that there's a young&nbsp;
electrician movement, there's a young plumbing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:52.480
movement and all these other things. They're going&nbsp;
to fix this problem of having to flush toilets 10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:56.640
to 15 times. I know that the young people are&nbsp;
going to be able to do it. But yeah, I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:01.680
listen, a lot of those jobs pay really well.&nbsp;
They are great opportunities. And when we talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:06.000
about making education more affordable, when we&nbsp;
talk about student loans, when we talk all these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:11.520
are things we should be talking about trades&nbsp;
just as much as we're talking about, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:17.280
traditional four year bachelor's degrees, 100%.
Speaker 4: Cool. Thanks. I was just going to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:21.600
like with something like this, be agreed upon&nbsp;
by people on both sides of the political aisle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:25.280
And so if a candidate were to just develop&nbsp;
this as part of their platform, would that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:31.280
you know, potentially garner them more support?
Speaker 1: I think so. The problem is just saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:36.320
let's let's pump up trade school wouldn't&nbsp;
be enough for either side. And so if you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:40.240
on the left and you go, let's pump up trade&nbsp;
school and do some student loan forgiveness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:43.840
then the right will say, well, well, well, we&nbsp;
can't do the student loan forgiveness. So I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:50.560
think that one part would have significant appeal.&nbsp;
But the question is what else is part of it? And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:54.149
that's where the disagreements would start.
Speaker 3: Right. Right. All right.&nbsp;

00:02:54.149 --> 00:02:57.109
Speaker 4: Well, I have another&nbsp;
question, but if you're good, I'm good.&nbsp;

00:02:57.109 --> 00:03:00.400
